<!--
  Pocket Prose's launcher icon: a closed notebook seen from above and slightly to one side, with a
  black ribbon marker laid across the cover and folded over the fore edge, resting on a pale disc.

  Generated from assets/icon_mk7.svg, which is the editable source and lives beside this repository
  rather than in it. Re-export from there; do not hand-edit the path data below.

  TWO THINGS ARE STRIPPED FROM THE SOURCE on the way in, and both must stay stripped.

  The rounded rect. mk7 bakes its own tile — a 779x779 rect, rx 151, filled with a vertical gradient
  — because the source is drawn as a finished icon. An adaptive icon keeps its ground in a separate
  layer, so the rect is dropped here and its gradient lives in drawable/ic_launcher_background.xml
  instead. Left in, it would draw a rounded square *inside* whatever shape the launcher masks to. The
  two files must be changed together. mk6 had the same rect in flat #0C0B0B; mk7 changed only that,
  and the artwork below is mk6's to the coordinate.

  The path with no fill attribute, duplicating the cover outline. SVG inherits fill="none" from the
  root there, so it draws nothing; it is dropped rather than exported as an opaque black shape,
  which is what a naive conversion produces. It cost an hour twice: the same trap catches any
  renderer handed the paths without the root element, including the comparison renders made while
  choosing mk5.

  WHAT MK6 CHANGED. mk5 solved a contrast problem by lighting one edge — #907878 at 4.28:1 — because
  dark artwork on a near-black tile has nowhere to go. mk6 solves it at the root by putting a pale
  disc under the whole mark, so every dark element has something to be dark against: the #3F3839
  edges read 6.87:1 on the disc where they read 1.53:1 on the tile. That also makes the artwork
  usable with no tile at all, which is what ic_mark.xml is for — see it for the in-app use.

  The disc is #D3C6C1 rather than the source's #CCC9C0. Same luminance, hue moved onto the app's own
  warm-mauve axis (AccentDark #AE9E98, ImagePaper #E5DDDF); #CCC9C0 reads olive beside them. The
  numbers are unchanged by the move: dark edge 6.87:1, cover 4.08:1, and the paper 1.41:1, which is
  the weak one — the white sheet is the icon's brightest element and it has the least room. Do not
  darken the disc without re-checking that number; do not lighten it without re-checking the paper.

  SIZE. This is the number that has been got wrong twice, so read the whole paragraph before
  changing it. The layers are 108dp, but a launcher only ever shows the **centre 72dp** — the outer
  18dp on each side is bleed for parallax and masking. So the tile you see is 72dp, not 108, and any
  scale reasoned against 108 comes out half again too big.

  mk6 is drawn as a finished icon, so the whole 779-unit canvas *is* the visible tile: it maps to
  72dp, and the scale is 72/779 = 0.092426 with the canvas centred, which is the whole of the group
  transform below. Nothing needs measuring here, unlike mk5 — that is the advantage of a source that
  includes its own ground. The disc lands at 55.4dp of the 72, leaving a ring of tile all round, and
  the notebook at 60.4dp against mk5's 62dp, so the mark is the same size on a home screen as it was.

  Three scales were rendered at 56dp, which is what this actually occupies on the Fairphone. The
  disc at 66dp (the safe-zone diameter) and at 72dp were both tried: 72 puts the disc's edge exactly
  on the boundary where masks differ, so the ring survives as four dark nicks on a squircle and not
  at all on a circle, which reads as an accident. 55.4 is the source's own proportion and the only
  one where the ring is unambiguously a ring.

  CENTRING is (108 - 72) / 2 = 18 on both axes, because the canvas is square and the artwork's
  placement within it is the source's business rather than this file's. mk5 had to subtract the
  drawing's own origin inside its canvas and got it wrong once; mk6 cannot, since it centres itself.
-->
